  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 135 AM CDT Mon Sep 21 2026\n\nThe surface cold front has settled south of the area across \ndownstate IL/IN and the Ohio Valley this morning, while a broad \narea of surface high pressure was building across western \nOntario and the far northern Great Lakes. Though the surface \nfrontal zone is well south of the area, the forecast area \nremains beneath the northern periphery of the associated \nelevated baroclinic zone. Several minor mid-level vort centers \nare expected to propagate east along the upper level frontal \nzone today, which combined with lingering moisture and transient\nweak mid-level f-gen will result in persistent cloud cover and \nsome light drizzle and spotty showers at times today. Breezy \nnortheast surface winds gusting 25 to 30 mph will result in \ncooler than average temperatures in the 60s, and will also \nmaintain high waves and hazardous swim conditions at our Lake \nMichigan beaches for the next several days.\n\nShower chances will continue (or redevelop) mainly west of the \nFox Valley across northern IL tonight into Tuesday morning, as \nan upper level low drifts east from the Plains to the western \nGreat Lakes. The eastward extent of these showers looks to be \nslowed/blocked by the continued feed of gradually drier low-\nlevel air thanks to the northeast surface winds.\n\nThe upper low will then gradually drift east-southeast across \nIN/OH and Tuesday night through Wednesday, while the \nsouthwestern periphery of the surface high to our \nnorth/northeast settles across the western Lakes and brings more\nsubstantially drier air to the area during the mid-late week \nperiod. Despite diminishing northeast winds, cooler than average\ntemperatures are expected to persist with daytime highs \ngenerally in the 60s and nighttime lows in the 40s (lower 50s in\nChicago). A gradual warm-up back into the low-70s looks to \noccur next weekend as medium-range guidance and ensembles \ndevelop upper ridging back into the upper Midwest. There remains\na signal for precipitation chances to return to parts of the \nupper Midwest late in the weekend, though a deep upper trough \nalong the East Coast may hold more meaningful chances west of \nthe area until early next week.\n\nRatzer",
